\description{
Computes one-step-ahead (OSA) residuals, also called forecast or quantile
residuals (Thygesen et al. 2017), for a fitted \link{Rceattle} model via
\code{\link[TMB:oneStepPredict]{TMB::oneStepPredict()}}. Unlike Pearson residuals, OSA residuals are
distributed iid standard normal under a correctly specified model even when
observations are correlated (through composition bins) or when the model
contains random effects, so they support objective goodness-of-fit testing
(Trijoulet et al. 2023; Stewart and Monnahan 2025).

These are \emph{internal} OSA residuals: the residualization is integrated into
the assessment via TMB, so it also accounts for correlation induced by the
model's random effects across years, the gold standard relative to the
\emph{external} \code{compResidual} approach (Stewart and Monnahan 2025).

OSA residuals are computed \emph{post hoc} and are expensive (TMB re-optimizes the
random effects as each observation is added), so they are not produced during
\code{\link[=fit_mod]{fit_mod()}}. The model must have been optimized with \code{estimateMode < 3}.

Supported observation types are the aggregate \code{"catch"} and \code{"index"} series,
the \code{"comp"} (age/length composition) and \code{"caal"} (conditional age-at-length)
compositions, and \code{"diet"} (predator stomach-content composition, for
multispecies models with estimated suitability). Diet is opt-in (not in the
default \code{types}) because it applies only to multispecies models and can be
expensive.

For composition data the multivariate multinomial / Dirichlet-multinomial is
decomposed into a sequence of univariate conditional residuals (binomial /
beta-binomial; Trijoulet et al. 2023). The final bin of each composition is
fixed by the sum-to-N constraint and so has no residual (returned as \code{NA}).
Composition OSA uses an internal model rebuild with unweighted, proper
densities (the \code{osa_mode} switch); fleets fit with the AFSC \code{MultinomialAFSC}
pseudo-likelihood are residualized under the full multinomial.

Survey-index OSA residuals are supported for every index likelihood family
(\code{Index_distribution}). Lognormal IID (\code{"Lognormal"}) residualizes on the log
scale, and the natural-scale \code{"Normal"} and \code{"TruncatedNormal"} on the natural
scale. The correlated covariance families (\code{"MVN"} / \code{"MVNORM"}) are whitened
by the lower Cholesky of the fleet's survey covariance Sigma = L L', so the
residuals are the multivariate-Gaussian one-step-ahead innovations
L^-1 (obs - q*pred), the closed form \code{\link[TMB:oneStepPredict]{TMB::oneStepPredict()}} reproduces for a
Gaussian block.

Under a Gaussian \code{method}, \code{"TruncatedNormal"} rows are residualized in their
own \code{\link[TMB:oneStepPredict]{TMB::oneStepPredict()}} call, with \code{method = "oneStepGeneric"} and a range
starting at zero. (Under \code{method = "cdf"} none of the rest of this applies:
the model supplies the truncated CDF in closed form, so the family is
residualized in the main call, exactly.) Its density differs from
\code{"Normal"} only by \verb{log Phi(mu/sd)}, which is a function of the prediction and
not of the observation, so a Gaussian method, which reads the curvature of
the density in the observation, cannot see the truncation at all and returns
the untruncated \code{(obs - mu)/sd}. Integrating over the family's own support
instead gives the truncated CDF
\verb{F(x) = [Phi((x - mu)/sd) - Phi(-mu/sd)] / Phi(mu/sd)},
so \code{qnorm(F(x))} is standard normal by the probability integral transform
however hard the truncation bites. The upper limit is finite rather than
\code{Inf}, ten standard deviations past the largest fitted index in the group,
which leaves under 1e-23 of the mass outside while keeping the Laplace inner
problem in a region it can solve. That group also runs with
\code{splineApprox = FALSE}, because the spline shortcut integrates over whatever
range its profile slice covered. The range is a
property of the family, so it cannot be shared with the other fleets:
\code{"Normal"} is genuinely untruncated, and a lognormal fleet's stored observation
is \code{log(obs)}, which is negative for a small index.

The size of the correction is the truncated mass: on a fleet predicting 100
with an absolute sd of 150 (a quarter of the density below zero), an
observation exactly at the prediction has an untruncated residual of 0 and a
truncated one of -0.44.

Three consequences of that group being residualized separately, worth knowing
before reading the output:
\itemize{
\item \strong{On a model with random effects the exact integration can fail.} It
evaluates the Laplace marginal at arbitrary values of the observation, and
the inner problem does not always converge across the whole support. Rather
than return \code{NA} for those rows, which would shrink the sample
\code{\link[=osa_diagnostics]{osa_diagnostics()}} passes verdict on, without saying so, the fleet is
recomputed under TMB's spline approximation and a warning says the residuals
for it are approximate. Fixed-effect models are unaffected.
\item \strong{\code{sd} is \code{NA} and \code{predicted} means something different for this group.}
\code{oneStepGeneric} returns neither a standard deviation nor the fitted value;
\code{predicted} is the truncated conditional mean \code{E[x | x > 0]}, which sits
above the fitted index (163.8 against a fitted 100.0 on the fixture above),
not the fitted index itself. The residual is unaffected.
\item \strong{Adding a \code{"TruncatedNormal"} fleet can still move the other fleets'
residuals on a random-effects model.} Each \code{\link[TMB:oneStepPredict]{TMB::oneStepPredict()}} call is
given everything earlier in the sequence as \code{conditional}, so a group that
is a contiguous block reproduces a single call exactly (measured at 1.5e-14
on a 21-random-effect fixture; without it the same split moved a residual by
5.8e-2). A truncated fleet's rows are interleaved with the other index
fleets by year rather than contiguous, so the rows falling inside its span
are still marked unconditional, which zeroes their data terms and changes
the conditional distribution of the latent states. Both orderings are valid
probability-integral-transform sequences, so no value is wrong, but catch
and lognormal-index residuals need not match those from an otherwise
identical model with no truncated fleet. Fixed-effect models are unaffected,
since their observations are independent.
}
}
\section{Choosing a method}{

The default \code{"oneStepGaussianOffMode"} approximates each conditional
distribution as Gaussian: it treats the observation as a free variable, finds
the mode of the conditional density in that coordinate, and standardises the
observation against it. That is fast, and it is what WHAM and SAM use.

\code{"cdf"} instead asks the model for the conditional CDF and returns
\code{qnorm(F(x))}, the probability integral transform, which is standard normal
whatever shape the conditional has. No conditional mean is formed, so the
method has three properties the Gaussian ones do not:
\itemize{
\item \strong{\code{predicted} is \code{NA} on every row} (and so is \code{sd}, which
\code{"oneStepGaussianOffMode"} does not return either). \code{\link[TMB:oneStepPredict]{TMB::oneStepPredict()}}
gives \code{Fx}, \code{px} and \code{nll} for this method and no fitted value, and there is
no conditional mode to put in the column; a marginal fitted value in its
place would mean the conditional mode under one method and the marginal fit
under another. Fitted values are in \code{fit$quantities} and in
\code{residuals(fit, type = "pearson")}. The column is blanked on the
Dirichlet-multinomial rows too, which run under a Gaussian method, so it
means one thing across the object.
\item \strong{The conditional mean cannot leave the support}, because it is never
computed. That removes the negative composition \code{predicted} values described
below, and the positive bias they pass into the residual on those rows.
\item \strong{\code{Index_distribution = "TruncatedNormal"} is exact and needs no separate
call}, so none of the three consequences listed above applies to that
family under \code{"cdf"}.
}

What it costs, in three places.
\itemize{
\item \strong{Not available for a Dirichlet-multinomial composition}: the conditional
is a beta-binomial, which has no closed-form CDF and cannot be summed at a
fractional count. Those fleets are residualized with
\code{"oneStepGaussianOffMode"}, announced in a message and recorded in the
\code{method} attribute.
\item \strong{\verb{|residual|} is censored at 8.04, in both directions.} The upper end is
forced: \code{\link[TMB:oneStepPredict]{TMB::oneStepPredict()}} recovers \code{F} as \code{1 / (1 + exp(.))}, which
saturates at the last double below one. The lower end is not, that same
expression takes a small \code{F} down to a residual of -37, and is censored
to match anyway, because an asymmetric ceiling would show as a long left
tail against a wall on the right, which is what skewness in the residuals
looks like. This is a ceiling, not a large number standing in for a larger
one: \code{\link[=osa_diagnostics]{osa_diagnostics()}} computes SDNR and the tail statistics on the
censored values, so it bites hardest on a short series where one
observation drives the statistic, and the function warns when any residual
sits there.

For an observation past the ceiling, \strong{reach for \code{"oneStepGaussian"}
specifically} and on the fleet in question, on a 12-year survey with one
observation multiplied by 200 it reports 38.98 uncensored, where the package
default returns \code{NaN} and \code{"oneStepGeneric"} compresses the same row to
3.33. It costs an \code{nlminb} and an \code{optimHess} per observation. The
comparison is in \code{vignette("model-diagnostics")} and reproduced by
\code{tools/verify/verify-osa-cdf-accuracy.R}.
\item \strong{Compositions are residualized in their own \code{\link[TMB:oneStepPredict]{TMB::oneStepPredict()}} call},
because \code{discrete} differs between them and the aggregate series and TMB
takes one setting per call. Everything earlier in the sequence is passed as
\code{conditional}, so the CONDITIONING is what a single call would have used.
The residual values are not identical to a single call under
\code{discrete = TRUE}: oneStepPredict re-seeds and draws \code{nrow(subset)}
randomizing uniforms per call, so a composition residual depends on how many
rows share its call. Set \code{seed} for reproducibility of a given call, and do
not compare individual randomized residuals across different \code{source}
selections. Fixed-effect models are unaffected by the conditioning.
}

\strong{Which is right for compositions.} Residualizing simulated data at the
parameters that generated it makes the answer exactly standard normal, so the
methods can be scored rather than argued about
(\code{tools/verify/verify-osa-cdf.R}, BS2017SS, 20 replicates, 4538 composition
bins each):\tabular{lllll}{
   method \tab mean \tab sd \tab lag-1 acf within a composition \tab KS rejects \cr
   \code{oneStepGaussianOffMode} \tab +0.103 \tab 0.918 \tab +0.060 \tab every replicate \cr
   \code{cdf}, \code{discrete = FALSE} \tab +0.610 \tab 1.262 \tab +0.434 \tab every replicate \cr
   \code{cdf}, \code{discrete = TRUE} \tab -0.007 \tab 0.995 \tab +0.001 \tab none \cr
}


The null standard error on the autocorrelation is 0.015. A composition bin
holds a count, so its conditional CDF is a step function and \code{qnorm(F(x))}
inherits the step; only the randomized quantile residual
\verb{qnorm(F(x) - U f(x))} (Dunn and Smyth 1996, the construction Trijoulet et al.
2023 prescribe) removes it. That is why \code{discrete} defaults to \code{TRUE} under
this method, and why \code{cdf} with \code{discrete = FALSE} is the worst of the three.
On the aggregate index and catch series, which are genuinely Gaussian, all
three agree (to 5e-5 wherever \verb{|residual| < 8}, i.e. everywhere the \code{"cdf"}
ceiling above does not bind) and all three pass.
}

\section{Random effects, where the choice reverses for the aggregate series}{

\code{"cdf"} is exact only on a fixed-effect model. With random effects
\code{\link[TMB:oneStepPredict]{TMB::oneStepPredict()}} integrates the CDF over the latent states by Laplace,
and the integrand there is a Gaussian times a sigmoid rather than a density,
so the approximation is not exact, where for a Gaussian observation the
Gaussian methods integrate a density and are. Against the exact Kalman
innovations of a linear-Gaussian state space model the Gaussian methods are
exact to 1e-14, while \code{"cdf"} errs by 7e-4 to 4e-2 as the latent state becomes
more informative relative to the observation
(\code{tools/verify/verify-osa-cdf-accuracy.R}, which compiles that model).

\strong{That result is about a LINEAR-Gaussian model, and does not transfer
wholesale.} Those methods are exact when the one-step-ahead \emph{predictive} is
Gaussian, which needs the model to be linear in the random effects. Rceattle's
index and catch are \code{exp()} of cumulated log recruitment deviations pushed
through the population dynamics, and are not. \code{fullGaussian} and
\code{oneStepGaussian} cannot differ for a Gaussian conditional, so their
disagreement measures the departure, on a 17-deviation fixture they differ
by 0.091 on both index and catch, while \code{"cdf"} differs from
\code{oneStepGaussian} by 0.017 on index. \strong{No method is exact for index or catch
under random effects}, and the choice there is not settled by this package.

\strong{\code{"ecov"} is the exception, and there a Gaussian method is right.} Its
conditional, a Gaussian measurement of an AR1 latent, every other data term
unconditional, genuinely is linear-Gaussian: \code{fullGaussian} and
\code{oneStepGaussian} agree to 4e-14 on the QAR1 fixture in
\code{test-likelihood-osa-cdf.R}, while \code{"cdf"} sits 0.139 away, about a quarter of
the residual standard deviation.

\strong{It does not reverse for compositions.} Their conditional is a discrete,
skewed binomial, which is what the Gaussian methods get wrong, and by far more
than the Laplace error above. Simulating from a 22-random-effect model with the
recruitment deviations redrawn and residualizing at the generating parameters
(1680 residuals, 120 replicates; null standard errors 0.024 and 0.017),
\code{"oneStepGaussianOffMode"} gives mean +0.513 and sd 0.404 with
Kolmogorov-Smirnov rejecting all 120, against mean +0.006, sd 1.002 and 6 of
120, the nominal 5\%, for \code{"cdf"} with \code{discrete = TRUE}.
The Gaussian default is not merely biased there; it is under-dispersed by a
factor of two and a half. What limits \code{"cdf"} on compositions is scale, not
random effects, see the section below.
}

\section{Known limitation, compositions at scale under random effects}{

On a random-effects model with a large composition data set, \code{method = "cdf"}
returns non-finite residuals in bulk and is very slow. Measured on \code{BS2017SS}
with \code{random_rec = TRUE} (159 random effects, 4538 composition bins):
\strong{1879 of 4538 residuals non-finite}, against 0 for
\code{"oneStepGaussianOffMode"} on the same fit, and hours rather than minutes.

The failures are a contiguous tail, and the same 1880 rows residualized on
their own return 1 failure, so the observations are not the problem. What
separates the two runs is the depth of the conditioning (2658 prior
observations against none): the Laplace inner problem fails on the
conditioning itself, and redoing the tail on a fresh call does not recover it
(1879 before, 1879 after).

What binds is the depth, not the presence of random effects: the same method
residualizes 1680 composition bins on a 22-random-effect model correctly (the
section above). So try \code{"cdf"} and read the warning it issues, when it
returns non-finite residuals in bulk, fall back to a Gaussian \code{method} for
that source, remembering that its composition residuals are under-dispersed
by about a factor of two and a half. \code{"cdf"} is sound on fixed-effect models,
and on random-effects models for the aggregate and covariate series, which are
few enough not to reach the depth where this bites.

One caveat that applies to every method, not just this one:
\code{Estimate_index_sd = "Analytical"} / \code{Estimate_catch_sd = "Analytical"} and
the analytical \code{Catchability} forms concentrate their parameter out of the
likelihood using \strong{all} the observations, including the one being
residualized. The conditioning is then not strictly one-step-ahead and the
residuals are approximate, by an amount that shrinks as the series lengthens.
}

\section{Negative composition \code{predicted} values}{

This section describes the Gaussian methods. Under \code{method = "cdf"} no
conditional mean is formed and \code{predicted} is \code{NA}, so none of it applies.

A composition \code{predicted} is an expected bin count and cannot truly be
negative, but it goes slightly negative where a bin holds almost no fish.
Composition observations enter as counts, \code{(proportion + comp_offset) * N},
and \code{\link[TMB:oneStepPredict]{TMB::oneStepPredict()}}'s conditional mean is a numerical step away from
the observation, which overshoots below zero when the count is near it.
The function warns, naming the count and the years.

It is the \emph{bin's} count that drives this, not the composition's sample size:
on EBS pollock the negative rows have a median observed count of 0.05 against
4.9 for the rest, while their sample sizes span the same 1 to 821 as
everything else (69 of 353 occur above a sample size of 100). A rare age in a
well-sampled year does it as readily as a poorly sampled year, so the warning
is not by itself evidence of thin data.

The values are reported rather than clamped, because a negative expected
count is the signal that the bin is too sparse for the decomposition to
describe and clamping would hide it. Treat \code{predicted} on those rows as
uninformative; their \code{residual} standardises the observation against that
same mean and is therefore biased positive.
}

\examples{
\dontrun{
data(BS2017SS)
fit <- fit_mod(BS2017SS, estimateMode = "Hindcast")
osa <- osa_residuals(fit, source = c("index", "comp"))
plot(osa)
}
}
